{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>There He made for him a statute and an ordinance.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): these are the statutes of the desert, for Moshe was king in Jeshurun, a leader, and he would rebuke them and command them how they should conduct themselves in the desert with the women and children, and also with the camps of the nations who would come there from distant lands to sell them many goods." "<b>And there are those</b> who interpreted 'there He made for him a statute and an ordinance' to mean that the Holy One, blessed be He, taught him the wisdom of plants and the powers within them, in their special properties and in their nature. For among them there are herbs that give life and herbs that kill, and among them are those that heal, and those that make sick, and those that sweeten the bitter, and those that embitter the sweet — all of this whether by nature or by special property. And this is the meaning of the phrase 'a statute and an ordinance': 'statute' is the special property whose reason is not known, and 'ordinance' is the nature, whose 'ordinance' — i.e., its due order — is that it be so in its nature." "<b>And there He tested him.</b> This plant. And therefore it says, 'If you will diligently heed the voice of YHWH your Elohim' — that is, even though the powers of the plants are known to you in their special properties and their nature, and you can be healed by them, it is not fitting for you to hang your trust upon them and upon their healing and to despair of praying and seeking mercy. You must not depart from the commandments of the Torah, for 'I am YHWH your healer' — and not the plants. And for this reason Hezekiah hid away the Book of Remedies, and they praised him for it, because with this book people would heal themselves of their illnesses and would despair of the Torah and the commandments." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'a statute and an ordinance' — the commandments given to them at Marah, and they are: Sabbath, and the red heifer which is called 'statute' (חק), and the civil laws (דינין). And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: Sabbath and civil laws at Marah were given." "<b>And if</b> you give heed in this passage you will find that here, at Marah, the wilderness begins: the prince of the wilderness began to prosecute against them and to make them sin, and he is the cause of their complaints. And this is what you find in Sefer haBahir: the Satan said to them, 'These waters — even though they are bitter, they are good, for they will suffice for you; but when you enter further into the wilderness you will not find even enough to wash your faces at all.' And further there: 'What is meant by \"and HaShem showed him a tree\"? It teaches that the Tree of Life was around the waters; the Satan came and took it from there in order to confound Israel and make them sin.' Immediately, 'and the people complained; and Moses cried out to HaShem, and HaShem showed him a tree' — that same tree was the Tree of Life which the Satan had removed. 'And he cast it into the waters, and the waters were sweetened': the Holy One, blessed be He, laid His hand upon the Satan and diminished him, and were it not so, Israel would not have been able to withstand him. As it is written: 'There He made for him a statute and an ordinance' — for the Satan; 'and there He tested him' — Israel. End of quotation. And therefore Moshe needed prayer to be delivered from his hand, and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him counsel how to escape — and this was by means of the Tree of Life, which is his opposite. And this is the meaning of 'there He made for him (שם שם לו)', hinting that Samael was 'there' (שם); and so Bil'am hinted in the verse (Numbers 24:23), 'Oh, who shall live when El does this (משמו אל)'. And this is clear."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {4 "Verse cited as Numbers 24:23; in standard numbering this is Numbers 24:23 in some editions, 24:22 in others — preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:59.196742643Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}